{"t":1794365447,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"08ae96f9-d61e-4ce9-9c41-7c8b12d8d490","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-11T02:24:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 110224\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n924 PM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast this evening. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow Friday\n  into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures \nfollow Friday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk \nconditions.\n\nDry and warm conditions are going to continue through Wednesday as \nupper troughing pulls offshore and weak ridging quickly traverses \nthe region. Another chilly night ahead as skies remain clear and \nwinds are calm across most of the area. Lows tonight settle in the \nmid 30s to lower 40s. A bit more cloud cover tomorrow keeps highs in \nthe mid to upper 60s, but that is still above normal for this time \nof year. \n\nA low pressure system passing through the Ohio River Valley and into \nthe Mid-Atlantic brings scattered showers across our region beginning \nThursday morning across the Alleghenies. Showers are expected to \nmove eastward across the area throughout the day. Depending on how \nfast this system moves offshore, precipitation could linger into \nFriday although primarily confined to the Allegheny Mountains and \neast of I-95.\n\nThe upper trough across the Northeast to OH Valley to Mid-Atlantic \ncuts off Friday, meanders south a bit on Saturday, then quickly \nflattens out Sunday into early next week as strong Canadian High \npressure builds in from the west. Behind Thursday's cold front, \ntemperatures are forecasted to be 5-10 degrees below mid November \nnormals. Reinforcing fronts are likely to cross the area through the \nweekend, maintaining cold temperatures and brisk winds. Combined \nwith ample cloud cover this brings us the season's first noticeable \ncold period.\n\nThe Allegheny Mountains are likely see their first accumulating snow \nof the season Friday into Saturday. A deepening low off the southern \ncoast of New England will produce northwest winds into the \nmountains, with moisture feeding in a favorable direction from the \nGreat Lakes. As temperatures cool Thursday night, rain transitions \nto a rain/snow mix, then likely all snow sometime on Friday. \nMarginal temperatures on Friday prevent much of that from \naccumulating, but that should change Friday night as temps drop to \nthe 20s in the Alleghenies. Elevations above 3000 feet are most \nlikely to see accumulating snow, but the marginal temps during the \ndaytime should keep snow totals on the low end (at least compared to \nwhat some of the model guidance is indicating). As the surface high \nbuilds in on Sunday, drier air should bring an end to snow showers \nby Sunday afternoon.\n\nChilly and brisk conditions continue on Sunday as high pressure \nbuilds to our west. Winds calm next week with temperatures remaining \nbelow normal.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /02Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions continue through Wednesday night. An area of low \npressure will move across the OH Valley to Mid-Atlantic Thursday \ninto Friday, then a strong upper trough brings brisk winds and cold \nconditions into the weekend. While some showers are expected \nThursday, they likely won't cause significant restrictions. Some low \nclouds are possible Thursday into Thursday night. The cold front \ncrosses the area Friday, then begins a period of brisk northwest \nwinds gusting around 20-30 knots Friday into Saturday. While winds \ndecrease a bit Sunday, the forecast still calls for gusts of 20-25 \nknots.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nExpect light winds tonight into Wednesday. An area of low \npressure will move from the OH Valley to Mid-Atlantic Thursday \ninto Friday, then a strong cold front moves across the area to \nstart the weekend. A prolonged period of SCA conditions is \nlikely to begin Thursday night and continue through Sunday \nafternoon. Most gusts are going to be around 20-25 knots, then \ngusts close to 30 knots are possible Friday into Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies are forecast to increase Wednesday into Thursday\nas south winds return. Sensitive locations could approach Action\nStage, with Annapolis possibly reaching minor flood stage during\nthe Thursday afternoon high tide. After that, a strong cold\nfront brings a prolonged period of offshore winds. This could\nresult in blowout tides into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/KRR/KVH\nAVIATION...KRR\nMARINE...KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794365395,"days":{"2026-11-10":{"hi":null,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Clear"},"2026-11-11":{"hi":67,"lo":49,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":64,"lo":41,"pop":38,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":51,"lo":37,"pop":12,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-14":{"hi":50,"lo":37,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-15":{"hi":52,"lo":35,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":53,"lo":35,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-17":{"hi":54,"lo":null,"pop":6,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794362400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,10600,null,15800,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5